SUMMARY:Olio Film Club: Hirokazu Kore-eda's After Life
DESCRIPTION:Join us for the very first screening of Olio's new Film Club!Po
 pcorn and wine included in your member ticket &lt\;3&nbsp\;About the film:
 &nbsp\;One of the most heartfelt and empathetic Japanese filmmakers workin
 g today\, Hirokazu Kore-eda ventured into the speculative for his 1998 sop
 homore film\, After Life\, where he introduces one of the most interesting
  concepts for the afterlife ever glimpsed in cinema: a way station of memo
 ry. In this fantastical feature with sometimes documentary sensibilities\,
  the newly deceased are interviewed and asked to choose the fondest memory
  from their lives\, after which the staff members of the afterlife are tas
 ked with building them—literally\, physically—as one would do for\, sa
 y\, the set of a stage play. It's in these nostalgic sets where the newly 
 deceased will then spend eternity\, or so we're told\, and thus these tran
 sient souls think long and hard about what meant the most to them when the
 y lived.The result is a moving film by a then-fledgling director who would
  quickly become one of Japan's most sentimental directors in the twenty-fi
 rst century with films like Our Little Sister\, Monster\, and\, very soon\
 , Look Back\, which premieres nationwide in January. An early but miraculo
 usly mature Kore-eda breakthrough\, After Life is both surreal and dreamy 
 in its imagination of life after death but still grounded in the reality o
 f what makes us human\, and right now particularly feels like the right ti
 me to watch it. The weather is getting colder in New York\, and the tumult
 uous year of 2026 is coming to a close. These days\, we might find ourselv
 es in a similar kind of introspection asked of the newly deceased in After
  Life even though\, of course\, we're still alive: We reflect on how the y
 ear went just as we ponder what we hope for the next\, and we can't help b
 ut think about\, for better or for worse\, how things are for us right now
 . Just shy of this New Year\, join us at the Lighthouse for not only a fil
 m screening of a wonderful film but also a moment of self-reflection.Hoste
 d by Olio member\, Bea Phi -&nbsp\;Bea Phi is a Vietnamese American film c
 ritic and editor based in Brooklyn. A longtime admirer and student of clas
 sic Asian cinema\, they hope to help introduce both its canonical movies a
 nd hidden gems to enthusiastic and thoughtful audiences in New York City l
 ike those in Olio.The evening:&nbsp\;Doors open at 7:00pmIntro and film ki
 cks off at 7:30pmChill and chat from 9:30ishTo join this event\, become a 
 member (at any level) which gives you two Olios each month for the price o
 f one. Clubs are by members\, for members.&nbsp\; They are smaller events 
 and benefit from having familiar faces who are committed to the community 
 in the room and we'd love to have you there! If you have any questions abo
 ut Olio membership feel free to reach out to us at hi@olio.rsvp&nbsp\;\n\n
 Tickets & info: https://www.viewcy.com/event/olio_film_club_hirok
URL:https://www.viewcy.com/event/olio_film_club_hirok
END:VEVENT
